I chose all negative gains because a stable linear controller for the inverted pendulum should apply a force opposite to the sign of each state component: push left when the cart is right (negative position gain), push left when the pole leans right (negative angle gain), and damp both cart and pole velocities (negative velocity gains). This “‑K·state” structure mirrors classic LQR solutions and directly counteracts the right‑bias observed in previous trials. The bias is set to 0 to avoid a constant drift. The magnitudes are moderate (‑1 to ‑5) to stay within the ±3 N force limits while providing enough corrective torque to keep the pole upright and the cart centered, aiming to significantly extend episode length toward the global optimum.
