I chose the signs of the gains to provide proper negative feedback on the most critical variables.  
- `params[0]` (cart‑position weight) is negative so that a positive displacement of the cart generates a leftward force, helping to pull the cart back toward the centre.  
- `params[1]` (pole‑angle weight) is negative, giving a leftward push when the pole tilts right and a rightward push when it tilts left, thereby correcting the pole’s angle rather than amplifying it.  
- `params[2]` (cart‑velocity weight) is negative, damping any drift of the cart by applying a force opposite to its motion.  
- `params[3]` (pole‑angular‑velocity weight) is also negative, providing damping on the pole’s angular speed and preventing over‑correction.  
- `params[4]` (bias) is set to zero so that the controller does not introduce a constant offset force.

Together these coefficients form a stabilising linear feedback policy that should keep the pole upright longer and reduce the systematic drift that limited the previous runs, moving us toward a much higher cumulative reward.
